PACK entity 1
-- THE ROOT CLASS (docs/c1f-layers.md, "One rule"). `Entity` is implicit: every entity's chain of classes ends in it, so a getter declared
-- here applies to every entity that has no nearer class answering. This pack owns it; a pack that wants another `summary` for its own class
-- declares `<Class>.summary getter: ...` (its class is nearer: it wins) and imports this pack only when it declares on `Entity` itself.
-- `summary` is what an entity IS in one line, from its id and its class list (`parents`; the eid's class when it has none) and nothing else (no property values: a getter
-- is eager, so the root getter stays as cheap as a string join): `Trip:main (cls:trip, cls:semnoun)`.
-- `updated` is the other thing every entity has and no pack defines: PROVIDED BY THE VM (native/src/vm.rs), read-only. `<entity>.updated` is the epoch time of the
-- entity's last write, stamped by the host in the same row and transaction as the write (the store's indexed `updated` column): no write of its own, no reaction, no
-- getter recompute (a getter that reads it keeps what it read until something else it reads changes). A setter that writes it is refused; a stored property of that name is shadowed.
Entity.summary getter: this.eid + ' (' + join(this.parents or [this.cls], ', ') + ')'
-- `Error` is the class of a FAILED entity, owned by this pack (the name is reserved for every other pack; docs/c1f-error.md). It has ONE typed property, `failed`, a dict {property: reason}.
-- The VM writes both: when a property of an entity fails (the value kind `Error`: a getter raised, the step budget, a failed cast, a Command timeout) it puts the class `Error` on top
-- of the entity's `parents` and the reason into `failed[prop]` (derived writes, never journalled); when every failure clears, `Error` leaves `parents` and `failed` is empty.
-- A read of a property listed in `failed` is the Error (`x is Error`, `why(x)`); the stored value stays null and the getter keeps recomputing, so a success takes the entry out.
-- This getter only DECLARES the property (its own name reads the previous value, so it never changes it).
Error.failed getter: this.failed or {}
